<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A transfer can copy photos, contacts or an app icon without proving that every local record, account session, authenticator, message history or provider service is ready. This guide is a planning and checking aid; it is not a substitute for an app provider’s or government provider’s recovery process.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="inventory"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Separate the jobs</h2>
<div class="mb-8 overflow-x-auto" role="region" aria-label="Phone and tablet continuity questions" tabindex="0"><table role="table" class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm md:text-base"><thead><tr role="row"><th scope="col" class="border border-line bg-surface p-4 font-bold text-ink">Question</th><th scope="col" class="border border-line bg-surface p-4 font-bold text-ink">What a copied device does not prove</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr role="row"><td role="cell" data-label="Question" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Are the files present?</td><td role="cell" data-label="What a copied device does not prove" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">That an app’s own records or cloud content finished synchronising</td></tr>
<tr role="row"><td role="cell" data-label="Question" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Can I sign in?</td><td role="cell" data-label="What a copied device does not prove" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">That a provider accepts the new device or recovery method</td></tr>
<tr role="row"><td role="cell" data-label="Question" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Does mobile service work?</td><td role="cell" data-label="What a copied device does not prove" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">That SIM/eSIM activation, calling or messaging is complete</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Make a private list of the accounts and apps that matter. Include authenticator access, banking, government services, messaging, email, cloud storage and any app with records that would be difficult to recreate. Do not put passwords, recovery codes, banking details or government identifiers into the list.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="accounts"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Check account-specific access</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Open each important app and check the result it actually shows. An app icon is not proof that its records, downloads, purchases, messages or account session copied across. A prompt for a code or a new-device approval belongs to that provider’s documented process.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For an authenticator, confirm that the household knows the provider’s supported transfer or recovery path before removing the old device. For a passkey, check whether it is synchronised through the platform account or stored on another device or security key; a copied app icon does not prove that the passkey is available. Keep the old device available until the new method has been checked. Do not disable passkeys or other security controls, and do not delete the old authenticator just to make the new phone look complete.</p>
{% call note("care", "Take care", "Do not improvise account recovery") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If access depends on an authenticator, recovery code, identity check or provider approval, pause and use the official provider process. A successful Wi-Fi connection or copied app does not establish account recovery.</p>{% endcall %}
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="provider"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Keep provider work separate</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Mobile-service activation, SIM or eSIM changes, porting and carrier account recovery belong to the mobile provider. Banking and government-service access belong to their official providers. Do not treat a generic transfer or a working Wi-Fi connection as proof that calling, SMS, banking or government access is ready.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If you perform an ordinary test of calling or messaging, keep it limited and never use an emergency number as a test. Record which provider step remains open rather than guessing that a device setting will resolve it.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="record"><span aria-hidden="true">04</span>Record what remains</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Use a short private list with four columns: item, what you observed, what remains unknown, and the next owner. Example: “Authenticator — app installed — new-device approval not checked — app provider process.” This separates evidence from assumption.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Keep the source device available while important account access, authenticator setup, messages or provider tasks remain unresolved. Do not erase, reset, trade in or discard it because a transfer screen says it finished.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="next"><span aria-hidden="true">05</span>What to do next</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Use <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/check-new-phone-before-erasing-old/">the new-device checking guide</a> for representative content and unresolved owners. Use <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/prepare-phone-tablet-transfer/">the preparation guide</a> before a future move. The official provider remains the owner of account, mobile, banking and government recovery.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">This article is educational guidance. The optional <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://nakedtech.au/services/phone-tablet-setup-migration-ivanhoe/">phone and tablet setup and migration help</a> covers one device and one working source device. You keep control of passwords, recovery codes, authentication prompts, identity checks and provider credentials; Naked Tech does not complete carrier, bank, government or account recovery on your behalf.</p>
